%N A397879 Largest integer of the form r^k whose base-n alternating digital root is r and whose number of base-n digits is k.
%C A397879 The alternating digital root of n in base b is the unique integer r in {0,1,...,b} such that n == r (mod b+1).
%C A397879 If n = (d_k d_{k-1} ... d_1 d_0) in base b, then the alternating digital root of n is the unique integer r in {0,1,...,b} such that r = Sum_{i=0..k} (-1)^i * d_i (mod b+1).
%C A397879 In base b, this sequence gives the largest integer of the form r^k, where 1 <= r <= b-1 and k > 0, such that adr(r^k,b) = r and the base-b representation of r^k has exactly k digits.
%C A397879 Every base has at least one such number, namely 1.
%C A397879 Also a(10) = A396781(10).
%e A397879 a(2) = 1, since 1 is the only number in base 2.
%e A397879 a(5) = 4^7. Its alternating digital root in base 5 is 4, and it has exactly 7 base-5 digits.
%e A397879 a(10) = 9^21. Its alternating digital root in base 10 is 9, and it has exactly 21 decimal digits.
%e A397879 a(16) = 15^17. It is the largest such number in base 16.
%o A397879 (Python)
%o A397879 from math import floor, log
%o A397879 def digits(n,b):
%o A397879     d=1
%o A397879     while n>=b:
%o A397879         n//=b
%o A397879         d+=1
%o A397879     return d
%o A397879 def a(n):
%o A397879     best=1
%o A397879     for r in range(1,n):
%o A397879         kmax=1 if r==1 else floor(1/(1-log(r)/log(n)))
%o A397879         for k in range(1,kmax+1):
%o A397879             x=r**k
%o A397879             if digits(x,n)==k and x%(n+1)==r:
%o A397879                 best=max(best,x)
%o A397879     return best
%o A397879 print([a(n) for n in range(2,17)])
%Y A397879 Cf. A396781, A394570, A397878, A010880 (for base 10).
